oscore — provenance note
=========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`adaptor`/`frost`/`bolt8`/`dtls`/`falcon`/`slhdsa` modules — self-contained
protocol/crypto modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

oscore — clean-room implementation of OSCORE (Object Security for
Constrained RESTful Environments), specified in RFC 8613 ("Object Security
for Constrained RESTful Environments (OSCORE)", Selander/Mattsson/
Palombini/Seitz, July 2019, IETF Standards Track). RFC 8613 is a public,
open IETF specification — a public spec/RFC's text is not copyrightable
expression (merger doctrine, CONVENTIONS.md §5); implementing it, however
closely, is not "derived from" any third party's code. This module was
built directly from the RFC 8613 text (fetched from
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8613.txt) — no third-party OSCORE
implementation (e.g. the C `libcoap`/`oscore-uip`/`californium`
ecosystem's OSCORE layers, or any other language's OSCORE library) was
read, ported, or consulted as a design reference for this module's Zig
code, types, or API shape. Every construction detail (the §3.2.1 `info`
CBOR array, the §5.2 nonce XOR layout, the §5.4 AAD `Enc_structure`, the
§6.1 compressed-option wire format, the §3.2.2 replay window) was
transcribed directly from the RFC's own text and cross-checked by hand
against Appendix C's official worked examples while writing this module
(see `src/kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc comment and each `root.zig` function's
doc comment for the specific section + worked-example cross-checks
performed).

`std.crypto.aead.aes_ccm.Aes128Ccm8` / `std.crypto.kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256` /
`std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256` (all MIT, part of Zig itself) supply the
AEAD, KDF, and hash primitives via their public APIs; no new AEAD, KDF, or
hash primitive is implemented here — RFC 8613's OWN construction on top of
them (context derivation, nonce, AAD, compressed option, replay window) is
this module's contribution, and RFC 8613 itself is the sole reference (no
NOTICE-triggering third-party design reference exists for this pass —
CONVENTIONS.md §5's "pure clean-room from spec, no NOTICE entry strictly
required" case).

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Official test vectors
----------------------

`src/kat_vectors.zig`'s test vectors are RFC 8613 Appendix C's OWN
published "Test Vectors" section (C.1 through C.8) — the spec's own
official conformance data, transcribed byte-exact from the RFC text (not
copied from any third-party implementation's test suite). Every hex field
in `kat_vectors.zig` was hand-verified against the RFC 8613 Appendix C
text at the time of writing (cross-checked arithmetically for the §5.2
nonce XOR construction — see `kat_vectors.zig`'s own doc comment).

**Status: scaffold.** The §3.2.1 `info` CBOR encoder (`encodeInfo`), the
§5.4 `aad_array`/`Enc_structure` CBOR encoders (`encodeAadArray`,
`encodeEncStructure`), the §6.1 compressed COSE option codec
(`OscoreOption`), and the §3.2.2 anti-replay sliding window
(`ReplayWindow`) are implemented and tested for real. The six crypto
cores — `deriveKey`, `deriveContext`, `computeNonce`, `buildAad`,
`protect`, `unprotect` — are `@panic("TODO(fable): ...")` stubs with their
final signatures fixed and their exact RFC 8613 construction fully
specified in `root.zig`'s doc comments and `SPEC.md`; `kat_vectors.zig` /
`kat_test.zig` are ready to validate a crypto-core implementation
byte-exactly the moment one lands. See `SPEC.md`'s "TODO(fable)" section.
